  "grammar": {
    "title": "Order food and drinks with confidence",
    "intro": "Whether it's a café or a restaurant, a few polite phrases go a long way. Learn to say what you <b>want</b>, to accept the waiter's <b>«¿Qué desea?»</b>, and to ask for <b>la cuenta</b> at the end.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "tag": "comida",
        "heading": "Food and drink",
        "body": "Everyday things you'll order — note the article and its gender:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "el agua",
            "water"
          ],
          [
            "el pan",
            "bread"
          ],
          [
            "el café",
            "coffee"
          ],
          [
            "el té",
            "tea"
          ],
          [
            "la leche",
            "milk"
          ],
          [
            "la fruta",
            "fruit"
          ],
          [
            "la ensalada",
            "salad"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "un <b>café</b> con <b>leche</b>",
            "en": "a coffee with milk",
            "note": "a classic order"
          },
          {
            "es": "una <b>ensalada</b>, por favor",
            "en": "a salad, please",
            "note": "ensalada is feminine"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "pedir",
        "heading": "Ordering: I want / I'd like",
        "body": "Use <b>quiero</b> (I want) or the softer <b>me gustaría</b> (I'd like) + the food:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "Quiero…",
            "I want…"
          ],
          [
            "Me gustaría…",
            "I'd like…"
          ],
          [
            "Para mí…",
            "For me…"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Quiero</b> un café, por favor.",
            "en": "I want a coffee, please.",
            "note": "simple and correct"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>Para mí</b>, una ensalada.",
            "en": "For me, a salad.",
            "note": "handy at a table"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "camarero",
        "heading": "What the waiter says",
        "body": "You'll hear these — and you'll ask for the bill at the end:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "¿Qué desea?",
            "What would you like?"
          ],
          [
            "¿Algo más?",
            "Anything else?"
          ],
          [
            "La cuenta, por favor.",
            "The bill, please."
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "— ¿Qué desea? — <b>Un té, por favor.</b>",
            "en": "— What would you like? — A tea, please.",
            "note": "a full exchange"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>La cuenta</b>, por favor.",
            "en": "The bill, please.",
            "note": "time to pay"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "gustar",
        "heading": "«gustar» vs «querer»",
        "body": "To order, say <b>quiero</b> (I want). Save <b>me gusta</b> for talking about what you <b>like</b> in general — and never say «yo gusto».",
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "<b>Quiero</b> un café.",
            "en": "I want a coffee.",
            "note": "ordering now"
          },
          {
            "es": "<b>Me gusta</b> el café.",
            "en": "I like coffee.",
            "note": "in general"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "To order, use quiero or me gustaría + the food.",
      "La cuenta, por favor = the bill, please.",
      "The waiter asks ¿Qué desea? — reply with «…, por favor».",
      "Watch the article: el pan, la leche, la ensalada."
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      {
        "w": "Yo gusto un café.",
        "r": "Quiero un café.",
        "why": "To order, use quiero. «Gustar» works differently: Me gusta el café."
      },
      {
        "w": "un ensalada",
        "r": "una ensalada",
        "why": "ensalada is feminine, so use una — not un."
      }
    ]
  },
